Activity # 3: Cybersecurity Workplace Communication Drill (GitHub-Based)

Objective
Practice real-world cybersecurity communication by working as a team using GitHub.
Students will analyze a cyber incident and communicate properly like a Security Operations Center (SOC) team.

Part 1: Access the Repository
Instructions
1. Your instructor will provide the repository link:

    cyber-soc-communication-lab
    https://github.com/xen0byt3/cyber-soc-communication-lab

2. Open the repository in your browser.
3. Review the following files:
README.md → contains the scenario
attack_log.txt → contains logs and evidence

Part 2: Analyze the Incident
Given Log File:
You will analyze events such as

    Multiple failed login attempts
    A successful login
    Privilege escalation (admin → root)
    A phishing email
    A user who reported entering credentials


Guide Questions

    Is there a brute force attack?
    Which IP address is suspicious?
    Was there unauthorized access?
    Is this related to phishing?


Part 3: Team Roles
Each student will be assigned ONE role:

Your role will be based on the first letter of your LAST NAME:

A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I → Tier 1 Analyst
J, K, L, M, N, O, P, Q → Tier 2 Analyst
R, S, T, U, V, W, X, Y, Z → Incident Responder

Team Lead: Instructor

Tier 1 Analyst
Responsibilities:

    Review the logs
    Identify suspicious activity
    Create a GitHub Issue

Your Task:
Write a short alert report using this format:

    Title: 
    Description:
    Initial Assessment:
    Severity:

[Example]
Title: Suspicious Login Activity Detected

Description:
Multiple failed login attempts detected from IP 192.168.1.100.
A successful login for admin was observed.

Initial Assessment:
Possible brute force attack.

Severity:
Medium

Tier 2 Analyst
Responsibilities:

    Perform deeper analysis
    Comment on the GitHub Issue

Your Task:

    Add findings such as:
    Timeline of events
    Confirmation of attack type
    Link to phishing activity


[Example]

Further analysis shows successful login after multiple failures.
Admin account accessed root privileges.
Phishing email may have compromised credentials.

Incident Responder
Responsibilities:

    Suggest solutions and actions

Your Task:

    Comment mitigation steps:


[Example]

- Reset compromised accounts
- Block suspicious IP addresses
- Enable multi-factor authentication (MFA)
- Educate users about phishing attacks

Team Lead (Instructor)

Responsibilities:

    Review all responses
    Approve escalation
    Provide final feedback


Part 4: Communication Rules

    Use clear and professional language
    Be concise and direct
    Avoid slang or informal words
    Focus on facts and evidence


Part 5: Submission

Each student must:

    Participate based on assigned role
    Post or comment in the GitHub Issue
    Follow proper communication format
